WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:04.940
Let’s be honest, you don't really need to
watch every movie in every popular franchise,

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:05.440
do you?

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:08.460
For one reason or another, some movies just
aren’t worth the trouble.

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.860
These are the popcorn flicks which franchise
fans are better off skipping.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.350
The Lost World: Jurassic Park isn’t exactly
a bad movie, but it is a little too long and

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:20.000
tends to feel like a band rehashing their
biggest hit.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.860
Aside from a few stellar action sequences,
the movie doesn't really move the Jurassic

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:30.090
Park saga forward, and the ostensible "payoff"
of a T-Rex running roughshod over San Diego

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.000
only occupies the final 20 or 30 minutes of
the film.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.680
And yes, a case could be made for Jurassic
Park III being inessential viewing, too.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:42.360
But at 90 minutes, that instalment is a simple,
dopey monster movie that doesn't ever wear

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:43.240
out its welcome.

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:48.040
And as the Jurassic films continue to stripmine
the tired "man must not tinker with nature"

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.680
trope, it's worth remembering that, in the
end, they're all dopey monster movies at heart.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:57.719
It's kind of a shame that X-Men: The Last
Stand is so very mediocre and skippable, because

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:02.719
the end of X2 suggested a powerful transition
to Marvel's classic "Dark Phoenix" storyline,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.280
with its own homage to Star Trek II: The Wrath
of Khan.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:11.000
Instead, the third film in the original X-Men
trilogy waters down Jean Grey's story by crowding

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:15.780
the movie with an endless array of unnecessary
villains, including a vast collection of old

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.700
and new bad guys from the Brotherhood of Evil
Mutants.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:20.740
"Don’t you know who I am?

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.660
I’m the Juggernaut, bitch!"

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.960
With 2011's X-Men: First Class, screenwriters
Matt Vaughn and Jane Goldman managed the tricky

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:34.680
feat of creating a film that was both a prequel
and reboot of the X-Men franchise.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:41.400
Its sequel, X-Men: Days of Future Past, adapted
a classic Marvel comics storyline which unceremoniously

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:44.200
retconned the events of The Last Stand out
of existence.

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:47.020
Miss this movie, and you won’t miss much
at all.

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:52.400
The Alien movies are renowned for their ability
to purvey an overwhelming, inevitable and

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.240
deeply uncomfortable sense of dread.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:59.520
Whether it's just Ripley and that single xenomorph
in the original film or the most recent cast

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:05.090
of colonial characters introduced in 2017's
Alien: Covenant, it’s usually a certainty

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.959
that there will be plenty of jump scares,
grisly chestbursters galore, and lots and

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:10.760
lots of death.

00:02:11.269 --> 00:02:16.100
Prometheus fails on a number of levels, but
it's that lack of any real dread that really

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.140
makes it the most skippable of Alien movies.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:23.920
Modern franchises may love to fill their universes
with obscure and often unnecessary origin

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.280
stories, and that may work for running up
box office numbers, but it doesn't make the

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.540
movies themselves any more interesting.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.480
Filled with backstory yet unable to strike
that sense of fear which makes Alien movies

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:38.180
so great, Prometheus just ends up feeling
bloated.

00:02:39.100 --> 00:02:42.980
If you're a Star Wars fan, you’re likely
familiar with the "Machete Order," a viewing

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:47.909
plan devised by fan Rod Hilton in which the
prequels slot into the original trilogy, right

00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:49.500
after The Empire Strikes Back.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:54.260
Instead of a tacked-on afterthought, the prequels
suddenly become a long flashback sequence

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.680
with far greater emotional resonance than
they originally had.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.820
Notably, the Machete Order omits The Phantom
Menace.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:05.099
The movie doesn't really contain any critical
story points relevant to the rise and fall

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:09.660
of Anakin Skywalker, or his eventual redemption,
so out it goes.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:14.860
Whatever order you do watch these films in,
it's very easy to omit the first Star Wars

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:17.460
prequel without missing any important story
at all.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.020
Of course, it doesn’t help that The Phantom
Menace is also a completely horrible movie.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.200
Chances are, it’s not only as bad as you
remember it, it’s actually worse.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.400
Just skip it.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:35.480
Terminator: Genisys was envisioned as a fresh
start for the Terminator franchise, arriving

00:03:35.499 --> 00:03:40.959
six years after 2009's Terminator Salvation,
which is today probably best remembered more

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.829
for Christian Bale's on-set rants than for
the movie itself.

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:48.040
Thor: The Dark World’s Alan Taylor stepped
up to direct, joined by Game of Thrones star

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.900
Emilia Clarke alongside Arnold Schwarzenegger
himself.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:55.599
After the project registered a disappointing
box office return, mostly because it opened

00:03:55.599 --> 00:04:00.469
in competition with Jurassic World, sequel
plans were put on hold and work started on

00:04:00.469 --> 00:04:05.740
yet another "soft reboot," which picks up
the plot after 1991's Terminator 2: Judgment

00:04:05.740 --> 00:04:06.240
Day.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:10.040
As it stands, Genisys might as well have never
happened.

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.080
"I’ll be back."

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:11.940
"What?"

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.970
2008’s The Incredible Hulk isn’t a terrible
movie, it’s just that this early attempt

00:04:18.970 --> 00:04:23.350
at franchise-building came along before Marvel
Studios had figured out the formula for making

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:24.660
their movies truly great.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:29.100
Coming mere months after the Iron Man, The
Incredible Hulk wasn't able to take on any

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:33.410
of Jon Favreau's tonal cues and suffered from
the need to escape the long shadow of Ang

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:36.020
Lee's disappointing 2003 Hulk movie.

00:04:36.410 --> 00:04:41.100
Incredible Hulk does have some interesting
franchise-wide backstory involving the super-soldier

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.700
program that gave birth to Captain America,
as well as a fun final scene involving Tony

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.980
Stark hinting at the creation of the Avengers.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.560
But considering that Mark Ruffalo would ably
take on the mantle of the Green Goliath from

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:56.880
Avengers onward, and considering the current
Marvel franchise marathon clocks in at well

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.420
over 36 hours, you shouldn’t feel too bad
for wanting to give this one a miss.

